evidence_beats.evidence:
Consider the loop accessing array `a`: 
```c
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < 100; j++) {
        // Access a[i][j]
    }
}
```
This loop accesses elements `a[i][0], a[i][1], ..., a[i][99]` sequentially for each row `i`. The observed 150 misses for array `a` implies that for each row, approximately half of the accesses result in a miss. Given an 8-byte block size and 4-byte integers, how many integers fit into one cache block? How many cache blocks are needed to store one full row of array `a`?

Now consider the loop accessing array `b`: 
```c
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < 101; j++) {
        // Access b[j][0]
    }
}
```
This loop accesses `b[j][0]` for `j` from 0 to 100, and this entire `j` loop is repeated 3 times for `i`. The total misses for array `b` are 101. Array `b` is stored in row-major order. How does the memory address of `b[j][0]` relate to `b[j+1][0]`? How does it relate to `b[j][1]`?
